1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,279
Episode Fifty-Five left us with a warmed-
up JVM: hot methods become native code,
and allocation keeps happening while that
code runs. The heap fills. Something has
to reclaim unreachable objects.

2
00:00:12,399 --> 00:00:22,359
The question is no longer "does Java have
GC?" — it is which collector should we
run, and what are we trading when we
choose? Imagine a checkout API under load.

3
00:00:22,479 --> 00:00:31,279
Requests allocate short-lived objects —
DTOs, temporary lists, buffers — while
some data lives longer: sessions, caches,
pools.

4
00:00:31,399 --> 00:00:43,000
Your product owner does not ask for "GC."
They ask for p99 latency under two hundred
milliseconds, or for a nightly batch that
finishes before the window closes. Those
are different goals.

5
00:00:43,119 --> 00:00:56,559
Collectors answer different goals with
different algorithms. Treat collector
choice as fashion — "what is everyone
using?" — and you skip the only question
that matters: what does this service owe
its users?

6
00:00:56,739 --> 00:01:06,619
Speak the trade space plainly. Throughput
is useful work over time. Latency is how
long requests wait when the collector
pauses threads or steals CPU.

7
00:01:06,739 --> 00:01:20,259
Footprint is how much memory and processor
time the collector itself consumes. You
rarely maximize all three. Tiny pauses
often cost concurrent CPU; batch
throughput often accepts harder pauses.

8
00:01:20,379 --> 00:01:33,019
Pick for service-level objectives, then
measure. Goals first, flags second —
otherwise tuning is superstition. On
modern server JDKs, G1 is a common default
for a reason.

9
00:01:33,140 --> 00:01:42,939
It is region-based: the heap is divided
into regions, and G1 collects sets of
regions rather than treating the whole
heap as one slab every time.

10
00:01:43,060 --> 00:01:50,900
That helps mixed workloads — lots of
short-lived garbage plus some longer-lived
data — without exotic configuration on day
one.

11
00:01:51,019 --> 00:02:01,579
Starting with G1 and learning to read its
logs is more honest than hunting for a
"best" collector the night before launch.
When SLOs are about pause time, low-pause
collectors enter.

12
00:02:01,699 --> 00:02:14,739
ZGC and Shenandoah chase short pauses by
doing more work concurrently with the
application. They are not free lunches —
they shift cost into CPU and footprint.
Latency-sensitive services with headroom
can win.

13
00:02:14,859 --> 00:02:26,699
CPU-bound batch jobs on tight machines may
lose: you paid for concurrency you did not
need and slowed wall-clock completion.
Parallel and throughput-oriented
collectors still exist for that reason.

14
00:02:26,819 --> 00:02:39,579
Batch jobs and offline pipelines often
care more about finishing than about a
pretty user-facing p99. A collector that
pauses harder but reclaims efficiently can
win on total time. "Newer" is not
automatically better.

15
00:02:39,699 --> 00:02:47,219
"Lower pause" is not automatically better.
Fit the tool to the SLO. Region-based
design is the mental model upgrade.

16
00:02:47,339 --> 00:02:53,419
The JVM tries to reclaim where garbage
actually is instead of treating every
collection as a full-heap emergency.

17
00:02:53,539 --> 00:03:06,379
Once you see regions, mixed collections
and evacuation read as "work on the messy
parts" rather than "boil the ocean." Make
the choice operational — evidence, not
vibes: Look at this bash for a moment.

18
00:03:06,499 --> 00:03:18,179
UseG1GC selects the collector. -Xlog:gc*
writes behavior you can inspect after a
load test or incident. Without the log,
choosing a collector is storytelling.

19
00:03:18,299 --> 00:03:28,939
With it, you see pause times, occupancy
after collections, and whether young or
mixed collections dominate — validated
under production-like load, not a laptop
idle heap.

20
00:03:29,059 --> 00:03:37,859
People fall down when they copy blog flags
for a different heap and allocation
pattern, chase zero pauses for a nightly
report nobody watches live,

21
00:03:37,979 --> 00:03:42,219
or switch collectors while an unbounded
cache grows the live set forever.

22
00:03:42,339 --> 00:03:50,299
The collector performs on the stage set by
allocation rate and live set size.
Changing the orchestra does not fix a
score that never ends.

23
00:03:50,419 --> 00:04:01,899
If an interview asks how you choose a
collector, answer under constraints: match
pause and throughput goals to the
workload, respect heap and CPU budget,
then measure under realistic load.

24
00:04:02,019 --> 00:04:11,979
G1 for a solid server default; ZGC or
Shenandoah when latency SLOs demand it and
you can afford the cost; a throughput
collector when batch wall-clock wins.

25
00:04:12,099 --> 00:04:18,339
Say what you will look at in the GC log —
and what you will refuse to change until
allocation behavior is understood.

26
00:04:18,459 --> 00:04:28,939
Today we moved from "GC exists" to "GC is
a product decision with algorithms behind
it." The next pain usually arrives after
you pick a collector and the heap still
climbs:

27
00:04:29,060 --> 00:04:31,740
objects that should have died are still
reachable.

28
00:04:31,860 --> 00:04:39,300
That is not a collector bug. That is a
reference leak — and the next skill is
finding those chains with dumps and
profilers.
